From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] lib/oofatfs: names on the API are UTF-8, so non-ASCII
 filenames work.
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FatFs was built with FF_LFN_UNICODE 0, which makes its API take and
return names in the OEM code page (437 by default) while MicroPython's
str is UTF-8. On a card written by a PC, a long name like "Boléro.mid"
comes back from os.listdir(b"/sd") as b"Bol\x82ro.mid", os.listdir("/sd")
raises UnicodeError, and open("/sd/Boléro.mid") gives ENOENT. A name
with a character code page 437 lacks ("Dvořák.mid") is reachable only
by its short name. Going the other way, a file MicroPython creates as
"Boléro.mid" appears on the PC as "Bol├⌐ro.mid".

FF_LFN_UNICODE now follows MICROPY_FATFS_LFN_UNICODE and defaults to 2,
UTF-8 on the API, so any name FAT can store works through os and open.
It only takes effect where LFN is enabled; FF_CODE_PAGE still decides
how short names are made. A port can set MICROPY_FATFS_LFN_UNICODE to 0
for the old behaviour.

A UTF-8 name longer than FF_LFN_BUF (255) bytes is listed by its short
name, as before for names the code page couldn't hold.

Cost: 472 bytes of code on the unix port (x86-64), no RAM.
---
 lib/oofatfs/ffconf.h | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/oofatfs/ffconf.h b/lib/oofatfs/ffconf.h
index 4a8015668..2146d84a8 100644
--- a/lib/oofatfs/ffconf.h
+++ b/lib/oofatfs/ffconf.h
@@ -164,7 +164,13 @@
 /  ff_memfree() in ffsystem.c, need to be added to the project. */
 
 
-#define FF_LFN_UNICODE  0
+#ifdef MICROPY_FATFS_LFN_UNICODE
+#define FF_LFN_UNICODE  (MICROPY_FATFS_LFN_UNICODE)
+#else
+#define FF_LFN_UNICODE  2
+#endif
+/* MicroPython's str is UTF-8, so the API takes and returns UTF-8 names by
+/  default; FF_CODE_PAGE then only matters for short names. */
 /* This option switches the character encoding on the API when LFN is enabled.
 /
 /   0: ANSI/OEM in current CP (TCHAR = char)
